From: Ilya I. Nikitin <ilya.i.nikitin@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] io: return buffers before reconfiguring the stream

The process pipeline copies each frame and then queued its buffer return
onto the io pipeline. A capture or the end of a burst switches the sensor
mode from inside an io pipeline callback: it syncs the process pipeline,
stops the stream and starts a new one, and only then do the queued returns
run. They requeue indices that the new stream has already queued, so every
capture ends in a run of VIDIOC_QBUF EINVAL errors.

Return the buffer directly from the calling thread. The process pipeline
is synced before every stream stop, so no return can race a stop, and
VIDIOC_QBUF is safe alongside the io thread's VIDIOC_DQBUF.

Blank frames dropped while flushing after a mode switch were never
returned at all, leaking one buffer per blank frame; return them too.

--- a/src/io_pipeline.c
+++ b/src/io_pipeline.c
@@ -211,19 +211,10 @@
         mp_pipeline_invoke(pipeline, capture, NULL, 0);
 }
 
-static void
-release_buffer(MPPipeline *pipeline, const uint32_t *buffer_index)
-{
-        mp_camera_release_buffer(mpcamera, *buffer_index);
-}
-
 void
 mp_io_pipeline_release_buffer(uint32_t buffer_index)
 {
-        mp_pipeline_invoke(pipeline,
-                           (MPPipelineCallback)release_buffer,
-                           &buffer_index,
-                           sizeof(uint32_t));
+        mp_camera_release_buffer(mpcamera, buffer_index);
 }
 
 static pid_t focus_continuous_task = 0;
@@ -407,6 +398,7 @@
 
                         if (image_is_blank) {
                                 ++state_io.blank_frame_count;
+                                mp_io_pipeline_release_buffer(buffer.index);
                                 return;
                         }
                 } else {
